WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.170
SPEAKER 1: In this
Cloud Minute, I'll

00:00:01.170 --> 00:00:03.503
show you how you can you use
Stackdriver Debugger to add

00:00:03.503 --> 00:00:06.030
logging statements to
a running application.

00:00:06.030 --> 00:00:08.910
I have a Python application
running on App Engine.

00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:10.440
I've also set up
this application

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:12.420
in Stackdriver Debugger.

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:14.760
To add a logpoint, I
click on the line number

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:16.650
where I want to add the
log and then specify

00:00:16.650 --> 00:00:18.015
the content of the log message.

00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:29.300
I can reference local
variables by surrounding them

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:31.430
with curly braces.

00:00:31.430 --> 00:00:34.250
I can also specify under
what conditions the message

00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:35.540
should be logged.

00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:37.610
I want to log every
time this point is hit,

00:00:37.610 --> 00:00:41.110
so I'll leave it to the
default, which is true.

00:00:41.110 --> 00:00:45.490
Now, when a user hits that path,
the message will be logged.

00:00:45.490 --> 00:00:48.157
I can see the
results down below.

00:00:48.157 --> 00:00:50.490
I hope you don't have any
issues that require production

00:00:50.490 --> 00:00:53.160
debugging, but if you do,
logpoints is another great tool

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:55.190
to have in your toolbox.

